// HTML trigger context (3 fixed-nav buttons, each scrolls to a section heading):
//   <nav class="hr-bottom">
//     <button id="section1Btn">Section 1</button>
//     <button id="section2Btn">Section 2</button>
//     <button id="section3Btn">Section 3</button>
//   </nav>
//   ... <h2 id="section1">...</h2> ... <h2 id="section2">...</h2> ... <h2 id="section3">...</h2>


// =====================================================================
// BREAKDOWN (every item you asked about)
// =====================================================================
// 1. Verbatim scroll call:
//      gsap.to(window, { duration: 1, scrollTo: { y: "#section" + (index + 1), offsetY: 120 } });
//
// 2. Plugins used:
//      - ScrollToPlugin (loaded via ScrollToPlugin3.min.js, auto-registers globally)
//      - NO ScrollTrigger in the JS itself (the demo landing page lists ScrollTrigger as a category tag
//        but the actual pen does not use it)
//
// 3. Easing: NOT SET — GSAP's default ease is "power1.out". The demo accepts this default.
//    For a reading-position restore, "power2.inOut" or "power3.out" reads as more "intentional/
//    confident" because it eases-in at the start (avoids the instant lurch). I recommend power2.inOut.
//
// 4. Duration: 1  (one second). This is the documented default in most GSAP scroll demos.
//    For a returning-reader restore over a potentially very long article, 1.0–1.4s is the sweet spot;
//    going below 0.6s feels like a snap, going above 1.8s feels broken.
//
// 5. autoKill: NOT SET EXPLICITLY in the snippet — but ScrollToPlugin defaults autoKill to TRUE when
//    the target is `window`. Meaning: if the user spins the wheel, drags the scrollbar, hits arrow
//    keys, page-down, or touch-scrolls during the tween, the tween is killed instantly. This is the
//    behavior you want for "restore my position" — never wrestle the user.
//
// 6. Pause-on-user-interaction logic: NONE in user code. It's all handled by autoKill inside the
//    plugin. You can hook into it with `onAutoKill: () => { ... }` to e.g. clear a "restoring..."
//    toast / state flag.
//
// 7. Offset: `offsetY: 120` — to clear the fixed `<nav>` bar at the top of the page (which has
//    padding: 1rem 10px around the buttons). For a reading restore by exact pixel Y (not element),
//    you usually omit offsetY because the saved Y already IS the absolute pixel position.
//
// 8. scrollTo target value: an OBJECT — `{ y: "#section1", offsetY: 120 }`. The `y` accepts a
//    number (pixel), a selector string, an Element, or the literal string "max". For useReadingPosition
//    restore you'll typically pass a number: `scrollTo: { y: targetPixelY, autoKill: true }`.
//
// 9. Callbacks: none in this demo. Available in your integration:
//      onComplete: () => setRestoring(false)
//      onInterrupt: () => setRestoring(false)        // tween killed (not via autoKill)
//      onAutoKill: () => setRestoring(false)         // user scrolled mid-restore — the one you want


// =====================================================================
// REACT PORT — Next.js (App Router) + @gsap/react useGSAP
// =====================================================================
// File: frontend/lib/useReadingPosition.ts  (or a new useSmoothScrollRestore.ts that this hook calls)
//
// Install once at the app root (or inside the hook, guarded):
//   import gsap from "gsap";
//   import { ScrollToPlugin } from "gsap/ScrollToPlugin";
//   if (typeof window !== "undefined") gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollToPlugin);
//
// IMPORTANT for Next.js:
//   - ScrollToPlugin touches `window`. Register it client-side only. Either:
//       (a) put the import in a "use client" module and guard with `typeof window`, or
//       (b) call gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollToPlugin) inside a useEffect / useGSAP hook body.
//   - Do NOT register at module top-level in a file that may be imported by a server component.
//   - If you already use `@gsap/react`, wrap the call in useGSAP so the tween is auto-killed
//     on unmount and respects the React 18 strict-mode double-invoke.
//
// Drop-in replacement for the existing line in useReadingPosition.ts:
//   - // window.scrollTo({ top: target, behavior: "instant" });
//   + gsap.to(window, {
//   +   scrollTo: { y: target, autoKill: true },
//   +   duration: 1.1,
//   +   ease: "power2.inOut",
//   +   onAutoKill: () => { /* user took over — stop showing the "restoring" indicator */ },
//   +   onComplete: () => { /* fire your "restored" analytics / clear flag */ },
//   + });
//
// Full hook fragment (useGSAP variant — preferred in this codebase):
//   "use client";
//   import { useGSAP } from "@gsap/react";
//   import gsap from "gsap";
//   import { ScrollToPlugin } from "gsap/ScrollToPlugin";
//   gsap.registerPlugin(useGSAP, ScrollToPlugin);
//
//   function smoothRestore(targetY: number, opts?: { onSettled?: () => void }) {
//     // Wait one rAF so layout/images settle (matches the existing hook's "before we try
//     // to scrollTo" comment at line 96 of useReadingPosition.ts).
//     requestAnimationFrame(() => {
//       gsap.to(window, {
//         scrollTo: { y: targetY, autoKill: true },
//         duration: 1.1,
//         ease: "power2.inOut",
//         overwrite: "auto",                 // if a previous restore is mid-flight, replace it
//         onAutoKill: () => opts?.onSettled?.(),
//         onComplete:  () => opts?.onSettled?.(),
//         onInterrupt: () => opts?.onSettled?.(),
//       });
//     });
//   }
//
// =====================================================================
// INTEGRATION NOTES specific to /Users/taha/Claude projects/LucifersDiary/frontend/lib/useReadingPosition.ts
// =====================================================================
//   - Line 119 currently does:  window.scrollTo({ top: target, behavior: "instant" });
//     That `behavior: "instant"` is intentional — it snaps without animation, which is the
//     correct fallback if the user has `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. KEEP A FALLBACK BRANCH:
//
//       const reduced =
//         typeof window !== "undefined" &&
//         window.matchMedia?.("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
//
//       if (reduced) {
//         window.scrollTo({ top: target, behavior: "instant" });
//       } else {
//         gsap.to(window, {
//           scrollTo: { y: target, autoKill: true },
//           duration: 1.1,
//           ease: "power2.inOut",
//         });
//       }
//
//   - The comment at line 96 ("before we try to scrollTo") already establishes the rAF/settle
//     pattern. Keep it — GSAP doesn't fix the "target Y is stale because images haven't loaded"
//     problem; you still need to compute target AFTER layout.
//
//   - The existing hook is the SAVE side (writes scroll %); the restore happens in the same hook
//     when a saved position is hydrated. Only swap the restore line, not the save loop.
//
//   - DO NOT use ScrollTrigger here. ScrollTrigger is for scroll-driven animations; ScrollToPlugin
//     is for animating the scroll position itself. The demo's category tag "ScrollTrigger" is
//     misleading — the pen does not use it.
//
// =====================================================================
// A11Y NOTES
// =====================================================================
//   - Respect prefers-reduced-motion (see fallback branch above) — animating ~1s of scroll
//     can trigger vestibular symptoms.
//   - autoKill: true is itself an accessibility win — it means keyboard users hitting PageDown,
//     Space, or arrow keys during the restore are NEVER fought by the animation.
//   - If the article has a skip-link or anchored target ID, prefer focusing that element after
//     the restore (focus + scrollIntoView with block:'nearest') for screen-reader users. The
//     visual restore is purely cosmetic for them.
//   - Don't trap focus or block keyboard input during the tween. You shouldn't need to — autoKill
//     handles it — but resist the temptation to add a CSS pointer-events:none overlay.

